# Line-ending policy (carried from MacLCII_MiSTer, retargeted to MacIIvi).
#
# Scoped to EXACT paths for the template-synced sources: sys/ blobs carry a
# historical mix of committed line endings (LF, CRLF, mixed) inherited from
# upstream; a broad *.v/*.sv pattern would force-renormalize them and produce
# huge whole-file diffs against the MacLCII/Template lineage. The pinned paths
# below are all stored LF, so the pin is churn-free.
#
# Effect: these files are treated as text and always stored/checked out with LF,
# regardless of the editor or OS (immune to core.autocrlf).
/MacIIvi.sv        text eol=lf
/sys/sys_top.v     text eol=lf
/sys/hps_io.sv     text eol=lf
/sys/osd.v         text eol=lf
/sys/sys.qip       text eol=lf
/sys/audio_out.sv  text eol=lf
/sys/emu_ports.vh  text eol=lf

# Quartus project files. Quartus on Windows rewrites these with CRLF on every
# compile, which otherwise shows up as a whole-file diff. Pinning to eol=lf
# makes git normalize the working tree back to LF.
/MacIIvi.qsf       text eol=lf
/MacIIvi.qpf       text eol=lf
/MacIIvi.sdc       text eol=lf

# Shell scripts — must stay LF: the Verilator sim + ghdl conversion run under
# WSL/Linux, and a CRLF shebang breaks bash. All current .sh blobs are LF.
*.sh               text eol=lf
